# The crypto primitives themselves (X25519MLKEM768, ML-DSA-65) are Kconfig
# symbols owned by the esp-pqc component:
#   Component config -> Post-Quantum Cryptography (esp-pqc) -> mbedTLS -> Hybrid PQC TLS
#   Component config -> Post-Quantum Cryptography (esp-pqc) -> liboqs
# Do not redeclare them here (duplicate Kconfig breaks CMake). The profile
# choice below selects which CA and port the example uses, and main/CMakeLists.txt
# checks that the esp-pqc symbols agree with it, failing the build if they do not.

menu "Hybrid PQC TLS Example"

choice EXAMPLE_TLS_PROFILE
    prompt "TLS crypto profile"
    default EXAMPLE_TLS_PROFILE_FULL_PQC
    help
        Which combination of key exchange and certificate signature the example
        expects to negotiate. This picks the trust anchor that gets embedded and
        the TCP port to connect to, and it is cross-checked against the esp-pqc
        crypto options at build time.

        Each profile pairs with one scripts/run_server.sh mode.

    config EXAMPLE_TLS_PROFILE_CLASSICAL
        bool "Classical: X25519 + ECDSA-P256 (no PQC)"
        help
            Plain TLS 1.3 with no post-quantum algorithms, for footprint and
            handshake-time comparison against the PQC profiles.

            Embeds certs/ecdsa/test_ca.crt, connects on port 8443.
            Server: ./scripts/run_server.sh ecdsa

            Requires CONFIG_LIBOQS_ENABLED=n, which also drops both PQC TLS
            options and keeps liboqs out of the binary so the footprint number
            is meaningful.

    config EXAMPLE_TLS_PROFILE_HYBRID_KEM
        bool "Hybrid KEM: X25519MLKEM768 + ECDSA-P256"
        help
            Post-quantum key exchange with a classical certificate. This is the
            profile large deployments ship today: the hybrid KEM removes the
            harvest-now-decrypt-later risk, while certificates stay classical
            because the public PKI has not migrated to ML-DSA yet.

            Embeds certs/ecdsa/test_ca.crt, connects on port 8445.
            Server: ./scripts/run_server.sh hybrid-kem

            Requires X25519MLKEM768=y and MLDSA65=n. Note that with ML-DSA
            support compiled out, mbedTLS can no longer parse an ML-DSA
            certificate at all, so the ECDSA trust anchor is mandatory here.

    config EXAMPLE_TLS_PROFILE_FULL_PQC
        bool "Full PQC: X25519MLKEM768 + ML-DSA-65"
        help
            Post-quantum key exchange and a post-quantum certificate chain.

            Embeds certs/mldsa65/test_ca.crt, connects on port 8444.
            Server: ./scripts/run_server.sh mldsa65

            Requires X25519MLKEM768=y and MLDSA65=y.

endchoice

    config EXAMPLE_HTTP_DOWNLOAD
        bool "Download a test file over the TLS session and check its SHA-256"
        default n
        help
            After the handshake, request /tls_testfile.bin, store the response
            body in LittleFS and print its SHA-256, so the run also proves the
            record layer moved a known payload intact.

            This needs a server that actually serves files. Start the matching
            profile mode with the download argument:
              ./scripts/run_server.sh ecdsa download
              ./scripts/run_server.sh hybrid-kem download
              ./scripts/run_server.sh mldsa65 download

            When disabled (default), the example still sends the GET and reads
            the response, but discards the body instead of storing and hashing
            it. That is the right setting against a plain run_server.sh mode,
            which uses `s_server -www`: that option ignores the requested path
            and returns a generated status page whose contents differ per
            connection, so hashing it is meaningless.

    config EXAMPLE_DOWNLOAD_EXPECTED_SHA256
        string "Expected SHA-256 of the downloaded file (hex, empty to skip)"
        depends on EXAMPLE_HTTP_DOWNLOAD
        default "27783e87963a4efb6829b531c9ba57b44f45797f6770bd637fbf0d807cbdbae0"
        help
            Compared against the SHA-256 of the stored file, turning the
            download into a pass/fail check. The default is the digest of the
            100 KB files/tls_testfile.bin produced by the generator command in
            the README, so it holds for any freshly generated copy.

            Leave empty to only log the digest.

    config EXAMPLE_USE_TEST_CERTS
        bool "Use bundled test certificate (skip hostname/IP verification)"
        depends on EXAMPLE_TLS_PROFILE_FULL_PQC
        default n
        help
            Use the pre-generated ML-DSA-65 test certificate in certs/test/ and
            SKIP the server hostname (CN/SAN) check, so the example connects to
            the test server at ANY IP without regenerating certificates. The
            server certificate chain is still validated against the bundled test
            CA (certs/test/ca.crt), only the identity/IP match is skipped.

            This is for quick local testing only and is NOT secure: a valid cert
            from the test CA is accepted regardless of which host presents it.
            Do NOT enable it in production.

            When disabled (default), the example enforces full hostname
            verification against WEB_SERVER and you must regenerate
            certs/mldsa65/ for your server's IP (see README, "Regenerating the
            ML-DSA-65 certificates"). Run the matching test server with:
              ./scripts/run_server.sh test

            Only available in the full PQC profile, because the bundled test
            certificate is ML-DSA-65 signed.

endmenu
